  "output": "Here's the full performance analysis across your codebase:\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis\n\n### 1. N+1 Query Patterns (High Severity)\n\n**`apps/api/src/server/notify.ts` — Sequential `notification.create()`**\n\nEvery notification is inserted individually in a `for` loop. With 50 @mentions on a post, that's 50 sequential DB round-trips.\n\n```typescript\n// Current: N round-trips\nfor (const row of rows) await db.notification.create({ data: row });\n\n// Fix: single round-trip\nawait db.notification.createMany({ data: rows });\n```\n\n---\n\n**`apps/api/src/server/plugins/realtime-plugin.ts` — Double group lookup per post**\n\nThe `Post` handler fetches `group.organizationId`, then calls `mentionedReaders()` which fetches the **same group again** for `privacy` and `organizationId`.\n\n```typescript\n// Post handler\nconst group = await db.group.findUnique({ where: { id: groupId }, select: { organizationId: true } });\n\n// Inside mentionedReaders() — queries same group AGAIN\nconst group = await db.group.findUnique({ where: { id: groupId }, select: { privacy: true, organizationId: true } });\n```\n\n**Fix:** Fetch both fields in one query and pass the group object to `mentionedReaders`.\n\n---\n\n**`apps/api/src/server/plugins/realtime-plugin.ts` — Comment handler: 4+ queries per comment**\n\nEach comment triggers: (1) fetch parent post, (2) `mentionedReaders` (which re-fetches group), (3) check existing notification, (4) check parent comment. For a busy thread, this is severe.\n\n---\n\n**`apps/api/src/server/plugins/realtime-plugin.ts` — GroupMember handler**\n\nFor each `PENDING` membership, it fetches group, then fetches all admins, then creates N notifications sequentially. With a large org, group joins are very expensive.\n\n**Fix pattern for all three:**\n```typescript\n// Collect all IDs first, then batch-fetch\nconst groupIds = [...new Set(rows.map(r => r.groupId))];\nconst groups = await db.group.findMany({\n  where: { id: { in: groupIds } },\n  select: { id: true, privacy: true, organizationId: true },\n});\nconst groupMap = new Map(groups.map(g => [g.id, g]));\n// Use groupMap[row.groupId] instead of per-row queries\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unnecessary Re-renders\n\n**`apps/web/src/components/layout/org-layout.tsx` — Context value recreated every render**\n\nThis is the most impactful re-render issue — it causes *every* `useOrg()` consumer to re-render on any parent state change.\n\n```typescript\n// Current: new object every render → all consumers re-render\nconst value = { org, me: session.user, role, isAdmin, href: (path) => ... };\n\n// Fix\nconst value = useMemo(() => ({\n  org, me: session.user, role,\n  isAdmin: role === \"owner\" || role === \"admin\",\n  href: (path = \"\") => `/${org.slug}${path === \"/\" ? \"\" : path}`,\n}), [org, session.user, role]);\n```\n\n---\n\n**`apps/web/src/components/chat/message-row.tsx` — Inline array derivations**\n\nWith 50+ visible messages, these fire on every render:\n\n```typescript\n// Each fires on every render — add useMemo:\nconst reactionGroups = useMemo(() =>\n  REACTIONS.map((r) => ({\n    ...r,\n    users: message.reactions.filter((x) => x.type === r.type),\n  })).filter((g) => g.users.length > 0),\n  [message.reactions]\n);\n\nconst myReaction = useMemo(() =>\n  message.reactions.find((r) => r.userId === me.id)?.type,\n  [message.reactions, me.id]\n);\n```\n\n---\n\n**`apps/web/src/components/feed/post-card.tsx` — Not wrapped in `React.memo`**\n\nPostCard is a heavy component (rich text, comments, polls, reactions). It re-renders every time the feed parent scrolls or updates. Wrap it:\n\n```typescript\nexport const PostCard = React.memo(function PostCard({ post, ...props }) {\n  // ...\n}, (prev, next) => prev.post.id === next.post.id && prev.showGroup === next.showGroup);\n```\n\n---\n\n**`apps/web/src/components/call/call-provider.tsx` — Context value and inline handlers**\n\n```typescript\n// Current: new object every render\n<CallsContext.Provider value={{ startCall, joinCall }}>\n\n// Fix\nconst contextValue = useMemo(() => ({ startCall, joinCall }), [startCall, joinCall]);\nconst handleAccept = useCallback(() => current && accept(current), [current, accept]);\nconst handleDecline = useCallback(() => current && decline(current), [current, decline]);\n```\n\n---\n\n**`apps/web/src/components/chat/conversation-list.tsx` — List rebuilt on every keystroke**\n\n```typescript\nconst rows = useMemo(() =>\n  (data ?? []).map((item) => ({\n    item,\n    title: conversationTitle(item.conversation, item.conversation.members, me.id),\n  })),\n  [data, me.id]\n);\n\nconst filtered = useMemo(() =>\n  term ? rows.filter(({ item, title }) => ...) : rows,\n  [rows, term]\n);\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Caching Opportunities\n\n**`apps/web/src/lib/query.ts` — Uniform 30s staleTime is too aggressive with real-time**\n\nSince WebSocket invalidates the cache on mutations, you can safely extend staleTime by model type:\n\n```typescript\nconst MODEL_STALETIME: Partial<Record<string, number>> = {\n  group:    5 * 60_000,   // rarely changes\n  user:    10 * 60_000,\n  post:     2 * 60_000,   // real-time handles live updates\n  message:  1 * 60_000,\n  presence:         0,    // always re-fetch\n  notification:     0,\n};\n\n// In useDbQuery, look up staleTime from key[0]\n```\n\nThis reduces background refetch churn for stable data.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Memory Leaks\n\nNo critical leaks found. The code follows cleanup patterns correctly:\n\n- `use-socket.tsx` — event listeners cleaned up in `useEffect` return ✓\n- `message-list.tsx` — window focus/blur listeners properly removed ✓\n\n**Potential issue in `apps/api/src/websocket/hub.ts`** — the `members` Map (room → Set<Conn>) and `connOwner` Map grow without a periodic sweep. If a browser crashes without sending a close frame, stale entries could accumulate. Mitigation: the `dispose()` callback handles normal disconnects, but a heartbeat-based prune is safer at scale.\n\n**`apps/api/src/websocket/calls.ts`** — `live` Map (callId → peers) is cleaned by `endCall()` and a ring timeout, but an exception in a handler would strand the entry. A defensive sweep is worth adding:\n\n```typescript\n// Runs every 10 minutes, cleans calls older than 24h\nsetInterval(async () => {\n  for (const [callId] of live) {\n    const call = await db.call.findUnique({ where: { id: callId }, select: { startedAt: true, endedAt: true } });\n    if (!call || call.endedAt || Date.now() - call.startedAt.getTime() > 86_400_000)\n      await endCall(callId).catch(console.error);\n  }\n}, 10 * 60_000);\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Redundant Computations\n\n**`apps/api/src/server/plugins/realtime-plugin.ts`** — `mentionedReaders()` called per post/comment row inside a loop, each re-querying the group. The group data is already available at the loop level (see N+1 fix above).\n\n**`apps/web/src/components/chat/conversation-list.tsx`** — `conversationTitle()` is computed inside a `.map()` on every render (see re-render fix above).\n\n---\n\n### Priority Order\n\n| Priority | Fix | File | Impact |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Batch `notification.create()` | `notify.ts` | 50× slower on @mention-heavy posts |\n| 2 | Pass `group` to `mentionedReaders` | `realtime-plugin.ts` | Halves queries per post |\n| 3 | Batch all realtime-plugin loops | `realtime-plugin.ts` | Group join/comment storms |\n| 4 | Memoize `OrgProvider` value | `org-layout.tsx` | Stops global re-render cascade |\n| 5 | `React.memo` on `PostCard` | `post-card.tsx` | Feed scroll performance |\n| 6 | Memoize `MessageRow` derivations | `message-row.tsx` | Chat scroll jank |\n| 7 | Model-differentiated `staleTime` | `query.ts` | Reduces background refetch churn |\n| 8 | Defensive call state cleanup | `calls.ts` | Prevents slow memory accumulation |\n\nItems 1–3 are backend N+1 fixes that will matter most as data scales. Items 4–6 are straightforward React memoization that can be done incrementally without risk.\n",
